\begin_layout Subsection
What the matrix must look like
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Storage.
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $H$
\end_inset

 is the module variable 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
H
\end_layout

\end_inset

 of 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
src/hamiltonian.f90
\end_layout

\end_inset

, declared 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
complex(dp), allocatable :: H(:,:)
\end_layout

\end_inset

. The main program allocates it as 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
H(Norb, Norb)
\end_layout

\end_inset

, with 
\begin_inset Formula $N_\text{orb} = 2N_\text{sites}$
\end_inset

, before your routine is called; your routine only fills it (do not reallocate it).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Basis order (essential).
\series default
 Spin-orbitals are interleaved by site: 
\begin_inset Formula \[
    2i-1 = (i,\uparrow),\qquad 2i = (i,\downarrow),\qquad i = 1,\dots,N_\text{sites}.
  \]
\end_inset

The rest of the code relies on this order. The Hubbard 
\begin_inset Formula $U$
\end_inset

 acts on the 
\begin_inset Formula $2\times2$
\end_inset

 spin block of each site, and 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
U_site
\end_layout

\end_inset

 is stored on the spin-up index. The first- and second-order self-energies loop over sites in steps of two. The spin texture, the populations and the spin-resolved current read odd indices as spin up and even indices as spin down. The lead couplings 
\begin_inset Formula $\Gamma_{L,R}$
\end_inset

 use the same order. A Hamiltonian written as two spin blocks, 
\begin_inset Formula $\begin{pmatrix} H_{\uparrow\uparrow} & H_{\uparrow\downarrow}\\ H_{\downarrow\uparrow} & H_{\downarrow\downarrow}\end{pmatrix}$
\end_inset

, must be reordered: element 
\begin_inset Formula $(H_{\sigma\sigma'})_{ij}$
\end_inset

 goes to 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
H(2i-1+s, 2j-1+s')
\end_layout

\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $s,s' = 0$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $\uparrow$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $\downarrow$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Hermitian.
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $H = H^\dagger$
\end_inset

. The easiest way is to fill only the 
\emph on
upper
\emph default
 triangle (column 
\begin_inset Formula $\ge$
\end_inset

 row, diagonal included) and finish with 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
call make_hermitian()
\end_layout

\end_inset

, as the template does. This overwrites the lower triangle with the conjugate of the upper one (and makes the diagonal real), so anything you wrote below the diagonal is lost. 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
info_Hamiltonian.dat
\end_layout

\end_inset

 lists 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{ij} - H_{ji}^*$
\end_inset

 for every pair; all must be zero.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Units and energy zero.
\series default
 Energies in eV, on the same scale as 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
mu
\end_layout

\end_inset

: at 
\begin_inset Formula $V=0$
\end_inset

 the levels below 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset

 are filled.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Static and non-interacting.
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $H$
\end_inset

 must not depend on energy or on the SCF iteration. The interaction enters only through the Hubbard 
\begin_inset Formula $U$
\end_inset

 of each site (Section
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:sigma"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

), so do 
\emph on
not
\emph default
 put Hubbard or Hartree terms into 
\begin_inset Formula $H$
\end_inset

: they would be counted twice. Fixed external terms (a gate potential, a magnetic field 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf B\cdot\boldsymbol\sigma$
\end_inset

, any form of spin–orbit coupling) are fine.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
One orbital per site.
\series default
 Each site carries one orbital with two spin states, and 
\begin_inset Formula $U$
\end_inset

 acts only between the two spins of that orbital. A molecule with several orbitals per atom can still be described by treating every orbital as a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

site
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 with its own 
\begin_inset Formula $U$
\end_inset

 (
\begin_inset Flex Code
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
hubbard_file
\end_layout

\end_inset

). The interaction between different orbitals is then neglected; this is how the DFTB+ models work (Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:dftb"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\series bold
Contacts.
\series default
 The left lead couples to every orbital of the atoms 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
lead_left_atoms
\end_layout

\end_inset

, the right lead to those of 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
lead_right_atoms
\end_layout

\end_inset

 (Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:leads"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

). For 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
custom
\end_layout

\end_inset

 an atom is a site, and the defaults are site 1 and site 
\begin_inset Formula $N_\text{sites}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout


\begin_layout Standard
Anything else is allowed. Hoppings may connect any pair of sites and have any range, on-site energies may differ, spin-flip terms may appear anywhere, and the matrix may be dense. The second-order self-energy is computed for every pair of interacting sites, so it does not assume any particular connectivity. The energy grid needs no change: it is built from the eigenvalues of whatever 
\begin_inset Formula $H$
\end_inset

 you supply.
\end_layout

